apple has finally revealed their m2 chip  for their new redesigned macbook air and  base macbook pro and a lot of people are  disappointed so i've been digging into  the technical elements of the m2 and the  more i dig in the more i realized that  this might be a stopgap m 1.5 chip with  a ton of apple marketing fluff hiding  the truth but don't worry the new m2 max  are still very good and there's actually  one way that the m2 is faster than the  m1 pro in the 2 000 14 inch macbook pro  but before i get into that let me first  go through everything you need to know  about the m2 chip including the  performance calculations and comparisons  now let me first give you guys a quick  rundown of the m2 chip it's basically a  larger version of the m1 with the same  cpu core layout with four performance  cores for efficiency cores but with two  extra graphic scores on top of that  apple improved the memory bandwidth by  fifty percent up to a hundred gigabytes  per second by giving it a maximum of 24  gigabytes of even faster lp ddr5 ram and  they also upgraded the neural engine  making it 40 faster than the m1 and  according to apple the cpu is 18 faster  and the gpu is 35  faster now all of that sounds really  good but wait until you actually dig  into the real numbers about five months  ago i made this video on youtube  estimating the performance of the m2  chip and the way i did it was by looking  at the difference between the a14 and  the 15 and then applying that to the m2  and the way i did it was by looking at  two scenarios with scenario one being  that the m2 chip is gonna be based on  the a15 cores built on second generation  five nanometer from tsmc and then  scenario two was that the m2 chip would  be based on the future a16 chip built on  tsmc's third gen five nanometer which is  known as four nanometer which the new  qualcomm snapdragon 8 gen 1 chip is  already on and unfortunately it looks  like scenario one is what apple went  with because they just confirmed that  the m2 is on the second generation five  nanometer which disappointingly means  that the m2 chip is based directly off  of the a15 channel so what this  basically means is that the method that  i use to estimate the performance is  actually fairly accurate and it matches  up to apple's own estimates of 18 faster  cpu performance and now looking at the  actual graphics performance my estimate  was around 28  874 points in the metal test and if we  add 35  to the m1 ship as you can see we have  around 28 580 points which is around 300  points off my initial estimate five  months ago so i'm happy about that but i  am not happy about this performance  improvement and before i dig in and show  you guys why this is so disappointing  let me first prove to you that the m2  cores are directly copy and pasted from  the a15 course and how do i know this  well first of all apple says the m2 chip  comes with a new neural engine with 15.8  trillion operations per second which is  40 faster than the m1 however if you dig  into it that same exact neural engine  already showed up in the a15 chip it's  literally identical to this one and it  was copy paste in but as for the nail in  the coffin evidence apple actually gave  us a real die shot of the m2 chip and if  we look really closely we can find  something very interesting first of all  dylan patel last year posted an a15 chip  die shot and annotation showing off the  actual performance cores the efficiency  course and the gpu cores on the a15 chip  compared to the a14 and as you can see  right here the image on the right is a  screenshot of one performance core on  the m2 chip compared to the a15 on the  left and if you look really closely  you'll see that they are literally  identical to the t copied and pasted  over from the a15 to the m2 not only  that but this is the efficiency core on  the m2 compared to the a15 and if you  look closely once again literally copy  and paste it identical and then finally  the graphics core a15 on the left m2 on  the right they are literally identical  once again copy and paste it and you  might be wondering why in the world does  this matter at all well it matters  because last year i did a full analysis  of the a15 ship compared to the a14 and  i concluded that it was mostly a  disappointing stop gap chip the a15  performance cores were only 7.6 percent  faster than the ones on the a14 and that  was mostly because apple simply boosted  the clock speed and added a bunch of  extra caches to help performance which  is exactly what they just did again for  the m2 so this right here is my estimate  for the single core performance of the  m2 chip and it is definitely  underwhelming now the multi-core  performance went up a bit more being 11  faster but that was mainly due to the  efficiency cores which was the real  upgrade in the a15 being 23 faster than  the efficiency cores within the a14 chip  and all of that is going to transfer  over to the m2 chip which is why my  estimate of the m2 chips multi-core  performance was almost bang on with  apple's 18  improvement claim and then finally the  gpu was perhaps the most disappointing  part because the a14 chip within the  ipad air 4 was actually quite a lot  faster than the new 4 core version of  the a15 ship within the iphone 13 and  that is due to thermal throttling of  course only the new 5 core version of  the a15 actually beat out the 4 core a14  in the ipad air and it gets even worse  when you divide the metal performance by  the number of cores and as you can see  the a14 chip within the ipad air  actually has more score per gpu core  than the iphone 13 pro max with the a15  chip look at that 3122 compared to  2887 and the reason that's important is  that if we take the score of  3122 from the 814 chip per core and  multiply that by five cores pretending  that the a14 was a five core chip we  would have gotten 15  610 but no the brand new a15 chip with  five cores is only 14 437 so there's  basically no difference and it's  suffering because of the thermal  throttling so basically the new a15 gpu  cores didn't really get any faster or  significantly more efficient and apple  essentially hid this by adding another  core to make it look like it's a lot  faster and guess what apple just did  this year with the m2 chip exactly the  same thing they added two more gpu cores  compared to the m1 so let's dig into the  numbers on the surface a 35 faster gpu  sounds very nice getting up to 28  874  points but that's actually disappointing  because the new m2 chip has 25  more gpu cores than the 8 core m1 champ  so it should automatically be getting a  25  performance boost even if they were the  same m1 course assuming there's no  thermal throttling involved so that  extra 10 bringing us up to a 35 gpu  performance boost is the real  performance improvement that we're  getting this year so apple did  everything they could to hide this by  adding more cores to the a15 which  they're doing to the m2 as well so with  that said the m2 chip is a stop gap kind  of like an m 1.5 with improvements that  are hidden by an increase in core counts  faster efficiency cores more ram and a  lot more caches and memory bandwidth and  the real upgrade is going to be the m3  chip next year built on three nanometer  from tsmc but don't get me wrong just  because it's an m 1.5 it doesn't mean  that these are bad macbooks they are  definitely still good and you still get  an improvement and it's definitely more  than powerful enough for a lot of people  and finally let's finish off with the  one way that the m2 chip is actually  better than the m1 pro chip the video  encoders apple said that the m2 chip  supports 8k h.264 hevc and pro res video  encoding and decoding which is actually  a huge improvement compared to not only  the m1 chip but also the m1 pro which  did not support 8k h.264 or hdbc and the  awesome part is that the encoders scale  linearly with the resolution so if it's  able to encode 8k video at 30fps it  should be able to encode 4k video at 120  fps which basically means that the video  exporting for video editors is going to  be a lot faster even faster than with  the m1 pro chip which you gotta pay two  thousand dollars to get basically  meaning that the m2 macbook air is gonna  be the best bang for the buck video  editing laptop for many people out there  but once again if you're not a video  editor then you're not gonna see those  media engine gains and as it turns out  the m2 chip is a disappointing stop gap  chip where apple is hiding the lack of  performance and efficiency gains by  adding more cores onto it so with that  said if you disagree with everything i  said in this video go ahead and comment  down below but if you enjoyed it click  subscribe so you don't miss out when we  actually buy all of these laptops and  compare them definitely check out those  videos right there thanks for watching  and we'll see you in the next video  [Music]
 

